<%= generated_header("Postfix virtual_alias_maps — address rewriting, applied BEFORE mailbox lookup.") -%>
#
# Order matters and is not obvious: Postfix rewrites through this map first, and
# only then looks the result up in vmailbox. An entry here whose key is also a
# mailbox therefore DIVERTS that mailbox rather than copying it. MailAlias
# refuses to store such a row and MailConfig refuses to render one.
#
# An "@<domain>" key is the catch-all. A domain whose policy is "reject" has no
# line at all — the ABSENCE of a key is what makes Postfix reject an unknown
# recipient, so there is nothing to write.
#
# A domain that FORWARDS its catch-all is preceded by one "user@domain ->
# user@domain" line per mailbox on it. Postfix's search order is user@domain,
# then user, then @domain LAST, so without those the wildcard would answer for
# the domain's own mailboxes too and divert every one of them to the forward
# target. They rewrite the address to itself, and vmailbox then delivers it.
#
# Plesk's own plumbing is deliberately not carried over — drweb@, kluser@,
# anonymous@ and mailer-daemon@ -> localhost.localdomain on every domain — but
# its one identity entry per mailbox was this same guard, and that part is kept.
<% alias_rows.each do |row| -%>
<%= row[:source] %>	<%= row[:destinations].join(", ") %>
<% end -%>
<% if alias_rows.empty? -%>
# (no alias configured)
<% end -%>
